Chapter: BoolKeywords
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `and`, `or`, `xor` and `not` as keywords (2026-08-19, Damian's direction).
 `&` and `|` keep working and nothing migrates; these are additive.

 THE PRECEDENCE IS THE PART WORTH A TEST, because it is the part a reader
 assumes rather than checks. `and` binds tighter than `xor`, which binds
 tighter than `or`, so `a and b xor c or d` groups as `((a and b) xor c) or d`.
 The keyword shares its level with the symbol it joins -- `and` with `&`, `or`
 with `|` -- so a file mixing the two spellings groups the same way either way.

 `not` TAKES A WHOLE COMPARISON, unlike minus. `not 1 == 2` is `not (1 == 2)`,
 which is True. The other reading, `(not 1) == 2`, is the one nobody means,
 and on Booleans it would type-check and quietly answer the wrong thing.

 `and` IS NOT `&` INTERNALLY. `&` is one token doing two jobs, decided by the
 left operand's type and carried to later passes as a span-table entry; the
 keyword is `OpBoolAnd`, which cannot be anything but boolean, so nothing
 downstream has to ask. `xor` and `not` need no operator at all: on Booleans
 `a xor b` is `a /= b` and `not a` is `a == False`.

Section: Entry

  show-b : Boolean -> Text
  show-b (b) = if b then "t" else "f"

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("and    " & show-b (True and False) & show-b (True and True))
    print-line-uni ("or     " & show-b (True or False) & show-b (False or False))
    print-line-uni ("xor    " & show-b (True xor False) & show-b (True xor True))
    print-line-uni ("not    " & show-b (not True) & show-b (not False))
    print-line-uni ("notcmp " & show-b (not 1 == 2) & show-b (not 2 == 2))
    print-line-uni ("prec   " & show-b (True and False xor True or False))
    print-line-uni ("mixed  " & show-b (True & False or True))
    print-line-uni ("bound  " & show (span-width 3 9))
  end

Section: The Bound Separator Still Parses

 `between` spelled its separator as the ordinary identifier `and`, matched by
 text in that one position. `and` is a keyword now, so the bound parser looks
 for the kind first; this definition is here because nothing else in the test
 tree would have caught that regression.

  span-width : Integer between 0 and 100, Integer between 0 and 100 -> Integer
  span-width (lo) (hi) = hi - lo
